WEBVTT

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:01.990
Last video ended with this fence.

00:00:02.352 --> 00:00:03.959
We'll solve it later, once we can build

00:00:03.981 --> 00:00:05.608
a function from a situation like this.

00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:08.661
First, there's a gap in our derivative list.

00:00:09.104 --> 00:00:11.150
We know sine and cos, but not tan

00:00:11.271 --> 00:00:11.411
x.

00:00:12.174 --> 00:00:13.481
Here's where tan turns up.

00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:16.337
A lighthouse stands one kilometre out from a

00:00:16.399 --> 00:00:17.504
long, straight beach.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:20.355
Turn its beam x radians from pointing straight

00:00:20.416 --> 00:00:22.603
in, and the lit spot lands tan x

00:00:22.644 --> 00:00:23.907
kilometres along the sand.

00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:25.316
Here's its graph.

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:28.890
It runs between vertical asymptotes, walls the curve

00:00:28.951 --> 00:00:31.559
never meets, at minus pi over two and

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.301
pi over two.

00:00:32.965 --> 00:00:34.837
There, the beam runs parallel to the beach.

00:00:35.645 --> 00:00:36.640
Watch its tangent.

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:38.904
Coming in from the left it's steep.

00:00:39.307 --> 00:00:41.635
It eases to its flattest at zero, where

00:00:41.656 --> 00:00:43.765
the slope is one, then steepens again.

00:00:44.448 --> 00:00:45.953
It never gets flatter than that, and it

00:00:46.054 --> 00:00:46.615
never falls.

00:00:47.319 --> 00:00:49.325
So why is the slope never below one?

00:00:50.229 --> 00:00:51.557
And what is it, exactly?

00:00:52.128 --> 00:00:54.375
Let's measure some slopes on this middle piece.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:57.346
At zero, the height is zero and the

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:57.928
slope is one.

00:00:58.693 --> 00:01:00.723
At pi over four, the height is one

00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:01.829
and the slope is two.

00:01:02.594 --> 00:01:04.680
At pi over three, the height is root

00:01:04.721 --> 00:01:07.270
three, about one point seven three, and the

00:01:07.311 --> 00:01:08.033
slope is four.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:09.288
Look at the pattern.

00:01:09.999 --> 00:01:11.414
Height zero, slope one.

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:13.515
Height one, slope two.

00:01:14.121 --> 00:01:16.056
Height root three, slope four.

00:01:16.702 --> 00:01:18.754
Each slope is one more than the height

00:01:18.815 --> 00:01:19.196
squared.

00:01:20.062 --> 00:01:21.931
On the beach, the slope is how fast

00:01:21.972 --> 00:01:23.820
the spot moves as the beam turns.

00:01:24.323 --> 00:01:26.732
It's slowest straight in front, and the further

00:01:26.773 --> 00:01:28.981
along the spot is, the faster it races.

00:01:29.826 --> 00:01:31.719
Hold on to that, because the algebra is

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.625
about to explain it.

00:01:33.103 --> 00:01:33.325
Why?

00:01:34.331 --> 00:01:36.558
Tan x is sine x over cos x,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:37.561
a quotient.

00:01:38.303 --> 00:01:40.890
The quotient rule video split a quotient's slope

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:41.993
into two turns.

00:01:42.575 --> 00:01:44.629
The top's turn is the top's slope over

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:45.053
the bottom.

00:01:45.536 --> 00:01:47.662
That's cos x over cos x, which is

00:01:47.722 --> 00:01:49.326
exactly one, everywhere.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.356
That's where the one comes from.

00:01:52.427 --> 00:01:53.516
Now the bottom's turn.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.663
Between zero and pi over two, cos x

00:01:56.784 --> 00:01:57.384
is shrinking,

00:01:57.886 --> 00:02:00.108
and a shrinking bottom makes a positive fraction

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:00.609
grow,

00:02:01.111 --> 00:02:02.051
so this turn adds.

00:02:02.874 --> 00:02:04.757
It works out to sine squared over cos

00:02:04.778 --> 00:02:06.741
squared, which is tan squared x,

00:02:07.363 --> 00:02:08.083
the height squared.

00:02:09.006 --> 00:02:10.871
At pi over four, that's one plus one,

00:02:11.454 --> 00:02:11.614
two.

00:02:12.278 --> 00:02:14.128
At pi over three, one plus three,

00:02:14.632 --> 00:02:14.874
four.

00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:17.804
And near pi over two, cos x gets

00:02:17.885 --> 00:02:18.747
close to zero,

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.617
so the bottom's turn grows without any limit.

00:02:22.262 --> 00:02:23.294
That's the steep wall.

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:26.170
The syllabus asks you to show this result,

00:02:26.571 --> 00:02:28.620
so here's the proof, line by line.

00:02:29.423 --> 00:02:31.888
Tan x is sine x over cos x,

00:02:32.309 --> 00:02:33.793
and cos x can't be zero.

00:02:34.475 --> 00:02:36.741
The quotient rule gives cos x times cos

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:36.902
x,

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.991
minus sine x times minus sine x,

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:42.137
all over cos squared x.

00:02:42.779 --> 00:02:44.863
The two minus signs make a plus, so

00:02:44.904 --> 00:02:47.549
the top is cos squared x plus sine

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:48.091
squared x.

00:02:48.755 --> 00:02:51.389
By the Pythagorean identity, that top is one.

00:02:52.114 --> 00:02:54.441
So the derivative is one over cos squared

00:02:54.603 --> 00:02:54.662
x.

00:02:55.689 --> 00:02:57.379
One over cos x has its own name,

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.043
sec x.

00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:00.611
So the derivative of tan x is sec

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:01.393
squared x.

00:03:02.398 --> 00:03:04.044
And cos squared x is never more than

00:03:04.206 --> 00:03:06.636
one, so sec squared x is never less

00:03:06.677 --> 00:03:07.017
than one.

00:03:07.643 --> 00:03:09.537
That's why the slope never drops below one.

00:03:10.028 --> 00:03:11.414
Two conditions come with this result.

00:03:11.978 --> 00:03:13.606
It's built from the derivatives of sine and

00:03:13.647 --> 00:03:15.757
cos, so x must be in radians.

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.007
And tan x only exists where cos x

00:03:19.149 --> 00:03:21.695
isn't zero, so x can't be pi over

00:03:21.756 --> 00:03:23.701
two, plus any whole number of pis.

00:03:24.745 --> 00:03:28.139
The graph breaks into branches, unbroken pieces between

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.063
neighbouring walls.

00:03:29.807 --> 00:03:33.235
Sec squared is always positive, so tan increases

00:03:33.337 --> 00:03:34.158
along each branch.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.896
But not across a wall.

00:03:36.701 --> 00:03:38.784
Tan of pi over four is one, but

00:03:38.825 --> 00:03:40.847
tan of three pi over four is minus

00:03:40.948 --> 00:03:43.171
one, even though three pi over four comes

00:03:43.253 --> 00:03:43.532
later.

00:03:44.114 --> 00:03:46.640
So tan x is increasing on each branch,

00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:48.245
not on its whole domain.

00:03:48.896 --> 00:03:50.770
Now a worked example from the course.

00:03:51.396 --> 00:03:53.601
Differentiate y equals tan of,

00:03:54.144 --> 00:03:55.848
two x minus pi over three,

00:03:56.411 --> 00:03:58.637
and find its gradient at x equals zero.

00:03:59.462 --> 00:04:00.406
First, the picture.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.595
Two x minus pi over three is two

00:04:03.656 --> 00:04:06.041
lots of x minus pi over six.

00:04:06.684 --> 00:04:09.353
So this graph is tan x squashed sideways

00:04:09.414 --> 00:04:11.822
by a half, then slid right by pi

00:04:11.843 --> 00:04:12.163
over six.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.096
You've seen this with exponentials.

00:04:14.758 --> 00:04:17.302
A sideways squash by a half doubles every

00:04:17.363 --> 00:04:18.484
slope at matching points,

00:04:18.986 --> 00:04:20.328
and a slide changes none.

00:04:21.032 --> 00:04:21.882
The walls move too.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.316
The squash pulls them in, and the slide

00:04:24.477 --> 00:04:26.805
moves them along, to minus pi over twelve

00:04:27.187 --> 00:04:28.350
and five pi over twelve.

00:04:29.362 --> 00:04:31.179
Now the algebra agrees with the picture.

00:04:31.662 --> 00:04:34.287
The outside is tan, and the inside is

00:04:34.368 --> 00:04:36.092
two x minus pi over three.

00:04:36.854 --> 00:04:39.526
The inside's derivative is two, because pi over

00:04:39.566 --> 00:04:40.631
three is a constant.

00:04:41.455 --> 00:04:43.881
So the outside gives sec squared of the

00:04:43.921 --> 00:04:45.565
same inside, times two.

00:04:46.529 --> 00:04:48.892
d y by d x equals two sec

00:04:48.933 --> 00:04:51.558
squared of, two x minus pi over three.

00:04:52.321 --> 00:04:52.928
Where does it hold?

00:04:53.491 --> 00:04:56.238
Wherever the inside isn't pi over two plus

00:04:56.278 --> 00:04:56.920
k pi.

00:04:57.783 --> 00:04:58.222
Solving that,

00:04:58.785 --> 00:05:01.369
x can't be five pi over twelve, plus

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.551
k pi over two.

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:05.201
Now x equals zero.

00:05:05.704 --> 00:05:08.177
The inside is minus pi over three, and

00:05:08.258 --> 00:05:09.443
cos of that is a half.

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:11.593
So sec squared is four,

00:05:12.137 --> 00:05:14.124
and the gradient is two times four, which

00:05:14.186 --> 00:05:14.566
is eight.

00:05:15.269 --> 00:05:17.876
The tangent at zero, minus root three,

00:05:18.398 --> 00:05:18.699
agrees.

00:05:19.288 --> 00:05:21.300
Here's a quick check for any gradient of

00:05:21.401 --> 00:05:22.064
tan x.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.782
The slope of tan is one plus its

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:25.567
height squared.

00:05:26.131 --> 00:05:28.677
That's just sec squared written another way, because

00:05:28.838 --> 00:05:31.083
one plus tan squared equals sec squared.

00:05:31.806 --> 00:05:33.969
At pi over three, the height is root

00:05:34.011 --> 00:05:36.355
three, so the slope is one plus three,

00:05:36.777 --> 00:05:36.996
four.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.993
It works wherever tan is defined, with x

00:05:40.094 --> 00:05:40.677
in radians.

00:05:41.302 --> 00:05:43.983
With an inside, like two x minus pi

00:05:44.024 --> 00:05:44.483
over three,

00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:47.546
multiply by the inside's derivative as well.

00:05:50.225 --> 00:05:50.694
Your turn.

00:05:51.201 --> 00:05:53.646
Find the exact gradient of the curve y

00:05:53.727 --> 00:05:56.192
equals tan x, at the point where x

00:05:56.372 --> 00:05:58.096
equals five pi over six.

00:05:58.883 --> 00:05:59.714
Pause here to answer.

00:06:10.548 --> 00:06:12.655
d y by d x is sec squared

00:06:12.737 --> 00:06:12.856
x.

00:06:13.499 --> 00:06:15.782
At five pi over six, cos x is

00:06:15.944 --> 00:06:18.748
minus root three over two, so cos squared

00:06:18.869 --> 00:06:21.272
x is three quarters, and sec squared x

00:06:21.373 --> 00:06:22.254
is four thirds.

00:06:23.117 --> 00:06:25.441
Check with the height: tan is minus one

00:06:25.522 --> 00:06:27.786
over root three, and one plus a third

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:28.668
is four thirds.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.015
The point is below the axis, but the

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:32.339
curve is still rising.

00:06:33.102 --> 00:06:35.223
Minus two over root three is sec x,

00:06:35.644 --> 00:06:37.705
not sec squared, and a tan slope is

00:06:37.866 --> 00:06:38.446
never negative.

00:06:40.273 --> 00:06:40.699
Last one.

00:06:41.289 --> 00:06:44.114
Differentiate y equals tan three x,

00:06:44.676 --> 00:06:46.459
and state the values of x for which

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.641
the derivative exists.

00:06:48.349 --> 00:06:49.221
Pause here to answer.

00:06:59.941 --> 00:07:02.966
The inside is three x, with derivative three,

00:07:03.387 --> 00:07:05.611
so d y by d x is three

00:07:05.811 --> 00:07:07.114
sec squared three x.

00:07:07.755 --> 00:07:10.417
It exists wherever cos three x isn't zero,

00:07:10.697 --> 00:07:12.799
so three x can't be pi over two

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:13.760
plus k pi.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:15.381
Dividing by three,

00:07:15.921 --> 00:07:18.203
x can't be pi over six, plus k

00:07:18.223 --> 00:07:20.345
pi over three, for any integer k.

00:07:21.186 --> 00:07:23.711
Copying tan's own exclusion, x not equal to

00:07:23.752 --> 00:07:26.197
pi over two plus k pi, misses x

00:07:26.338 --> 00:07:27.379
equals pi over six.

00:07:29.041 --> 00:07:31.326
So tan x, a quotient of sine and

00:07:31.386 --> 00:07:34.453
cos, has derivative sec squared x, on each

00:07:34.533 --> 00:07:34.894
branch.

00:07:35.757 --> 00:07:38.889
Next, the other three reciprocal functions: sec x,

00:07:39.231 --> 00:07:40.857
cosec x and cot x.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:44.289
Here's y equals sec x, which is one

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:45.112
over cos x.

00:07:45.876 --> 00:07:47.824
Its walls are where cos x is zero,

00:07:48.287 --> 00:07:50.055
and at x equals zero it sits at

00:07:50.256 --> 00:07:50.356
one.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.031
How does its slope change, and where does

00:07:53.071 --> 00:07:54.017
its derivative exist?

00:07:56.901 --> 00:07:57.704
Here are the key notes.

00:07:58.347 --> 00:08:00.075
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:00.136 --> 00:08:00.618
to copy them.
